#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b74aed1-5750-566e-973a-b9fe0109c876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995} {:answer/id #uuid "98ce5d05-688f-5cf5-9b21-352ade35d261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328254} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf58768-099b-5e1e-ba54-551e1eec0a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328289} {:answer/id #uuid "b440b75f-1cc0-592d-87f4-cd361fc03170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328501} {:answer/id #uuid "ef006457-c201-515f-919a-a5f8772d65f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b74aed1-5750-566e-973a-b9fe0109c876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327995} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fafe6c7-ed63-54d7-8bcd-351dbdd97823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7aef8bfd-11ab-51f5-ad27-2faedcadc29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48067" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c132c73b7ca4759882959f9df42110c36e31f3c8af27afa02c0caa20f5f94d52.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=f(1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1=f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18eaadf1-683f-5951-8da7-2c8b04aa572f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327151} {:answer/id #uuid "97125a5f-4f67-5544-94dd-ed787cd4805a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328242} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f71f4-41c8-5d9e-b405-045f8fa54bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328409} {:answer/id #uuid "f92419cf-94b1-5b46-bc59-f5aee0d957ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329120} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8882da-83f4-5fd5-a9fa-309e57cccf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa7f71f4-41c8-5d9e-b405-045f8fa54bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328409} :answer-field/id #uuid "d63a5833-0377-5a91-a4df-afbabcc49049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f506060c-db8a-5e26-aad7-74b6b6b44f61" :question/math-academy-id "q-48068" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a3874b7907227653d93451d9ce0960fdadc8cb3e10396e0f27f7ebb363bebe2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n- $f(x)$  is  continuous at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function doesn't have a break at $x=-1$ and we can draw the graph of $f(x)$ around $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2948f95d-327e-5b2a-ae44-d052e460ef7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333370} {:answer/id #uuid "4f47b504-2866-5cc0-a5de-126c425a1f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333715} {:answer/id #uuid "546b3906-9278-5c88-9389-7d8f880155e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333760} {:answer/id #uuid "c041a1ee-404c-5c71-88e2-cb1195daeaea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334655} {:answer/id #uuid "f1045889-a661-5c2b-b9ef-ba20d8522b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c041a1ee-404c-5c71-88e2-cb1195daeaea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334655} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa416a5-c692-5dfb-b0e9-591568885d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7573ddef-f834-5ca7-911d-588bad3a2d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-65307" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\nI. The domain of the function is all real numbers.\n\nII. $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n\nIII. The graph of the function doesn't intersect the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The domain of the function is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi ,$ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The graph of the function doesn't intersect the $y$-axis, as $x=0$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ Hence, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abfdb94-0b90-5fec-9454-e8ca2b729611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333236} {:answer/id #uuid "1f44d8a0-aa03-55c7-b38e-42f488e8b7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333270} {:answer/id #uuid "4600ff20-ec76-571f-b416-17d4739b6ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333638} {:answer/id #uuid "50e29691-496c-5d3e-bddc-fccb10414b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333727} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd338d2-6208-532a-8aee-5976f2d53a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1abfdb94-0b90-5fec-9454-e8ca2b729611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333236} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8851140-7924-5505-a58d-6ccd9f73c016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69d68c82-75d2-569e-bc5a-a35be8a71a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-65308" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is all real numbers.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\pi ).$\n- $x=5\\pi $ is in the domain of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi ,$ so $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$ Substituing $n=1,$ we get $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\pi ).$ Hence, Statement II is true.\n\n- The function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is defined for all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi ,$ so $5\\pi $ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ Hence, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c92f901-9b2a-5dd0-a311-5f44155934d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333561} {:answer/id #uuid "6efe0f16-e6b6-5943-b170-b88d45f78989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334000} {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f54b5-a442-5111-91a7-4f8042c17ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334116} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e9067e-96b6-5e89-980e-2099570a92ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334428} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0e7650-c97f-5547-8404-3506e873448e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e2f54b5-a442-5111-91a7-4f8042c17ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334116} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ba0ff0c-c72a-5014-bd52-d93eabbfcd56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2712841f-ac1b-5d69-a817-019e60c121da" :question/math-academy-id "q-66483" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=1-x,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73d9277c040ae457ba6d96e162574edbeeef965d181fa6011ca4ab0e4644bd82.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2043703a-2ac6-564e-91af-9ba34a032b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333279} {:answer/id #uuid "2451abb3-975d-5ee0-adeb-246bdac72fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333326} {:answer/id #uuid "88dc5451-9568-597c-bb95-1153a6238639" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334205} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5dda2d-b8ae-5a05-aaaf-ba2ec8110ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334365} {:answer/id #uuid "f33009df-84d6-5f5b-90d0-eaaebe0ab436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2451abb3-975d-5ee0-adeb-246bdac72fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333326} :answer-field/id #uuid "3294f802-41cc-53ad-b371-0f1a3c64773d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0655d813-1b1b-5f2a-9217-33c596819669" :question/math-academy-id "q-66510" :question/problem "![](images/38/3897b7fcf079b2522cbd0138631a894cbca1005cd241524cf9f3995965278612.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/95/95b1bd18e41cba7d6d1175f66be053ffea52a32fbb35e2ad1d126dbeb2d73baf.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- For graphs I and III, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I and III show one-to-one functions. This means that these functions are invertible.\n\n- For graphs II and IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057e6ce9-267d-57cc-ae69-ad84b7ee42ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333035} {:answer/id #uuid "2670d583-ba23-509d-984a-336387ded173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333342} {:answer/id #uuid "818226b6-2143-5b12-bb7a-35ba69eb59b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334139} {:answer/id #uuid "81f500aa-4f70-5107-844f-19fa4e38c1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334145} {:answer/id #uuid "9c018dec-8ad1-56e9-99a1-bc03b2303eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c018dec-8ad1-56e9-99a1-bc03b2303eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334363} :answer-field/id #uuid "66899f0d-563e-5ed9-a6cb-77e4a5b3f25b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26ae354c-e4a4-50d9-b820-8ba146650b10" :question/math-academy-id "q-66669" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (-1,5)$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/86/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c1e9df-cdaa-5e98-bb5a-91b82a10bb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} {:answer/id #uuid "253331ca-df9d-523a-8811-2487a95d3272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333334} {:answer/id #uuid "63786589-d496-56b9-bccb-1a7b14aff44f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333890} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfaecb8-b49e-5068-aef0-3b70aa437c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334362} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2e9670-55bb-542c-a3ec-ea63c48da4e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c1e9df-cdaa-5e98-bb5a-91b82a10bb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333202} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd33408-f473-5e92-b602-88c8f1b4211b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffb9d3e2-6b9a-5fb3-b64e-0b7d921759d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-66679" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n\nII. $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-1,3]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=(x-1)^{2}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y=(x-1)^{2}$  by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a4f97166877085c90acc01e3f8d0c6ac7bb3f704bbfb792d747122d314013.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/b0/b088c8ed1974a7c259dd54f85aec7a124579981971babfe8dee0313ca23b6458.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,3].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af13782a01213bb09fdeef38fbeb3fbca3f41348036e6786b4efa74a0166b4.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "289033af-746b-57e4-8f86-92f6034dddbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333366} {:answer/id #uuid "5a0ca728-76b0-59d2-8cf2-38f34d12ab44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ff2b4e-17d4-5465-aab7-ee5598712ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334542} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3233a-13a0-5b0d-bedc-05bdebf0a689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334587} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cbbd57-bce5-5d75-b881-127cf8bac14a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a0ca728-76b0-59d2-8cf2-38f34d12ab44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c83ae85-26c4-5f9f-9820-b59a10f14cd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1120070d-b929-5ac5-a6ba-244a40b57b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-66687" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Reflect its graph across  the $x$-axis to get $y=-{x}^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=-{x}^{2}$  by $1$ unit up to  get $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n![](images/83/83a9f5966c76b89b2cad9c6244e2e447fb0052fc7f946df954df76265628c4a7.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n*The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,0].$ On this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/f6/f694b6b1bc979289b748758385e05575990bd866457e75a84fbf7bdf335c03b9.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once. Since  $(-2,2]$ is a subset of $(-2,\\infty )$, our function is not invertible on $x\\in (-2,\\infty )$ either.\n\n![](images/c0/c03118f3fd0193910b49e26ab5ea56e4320fd23583feb3267cd4442d0a157583.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d39eb8e-2acf-5989-93c9-5706e83e7f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334106} {:answer/id #uuid "86eabbba-0a20-58a9-b168-1143669ceead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334184} {:answer/id #uuid "e1269f37-8751-57fd-a7c9-4c6a8b91e67d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334948} {:answer/id #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} {:answer/id #uuid "e693a8f1-2c29-5ee8-9bb7-6a65568289ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3884308-b6b1-5abc-b3d7-146aec3aae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0d2f2b9-b380-5289-b8eb-9732e9ebb27b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02e022db-b323-5e31-af64-39f79b6a6465" :question/math-academy-id "q-66725" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-x+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/aa/aaa05d1ab0fd630dacd81723fe00472cb901fb6f73af5f111f44459476191c0f.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09071e15-b470-5b7e-9b11-54f49cdedf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333072} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ccd7d-3b34-57fa-9756-01a55bf6d389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333408} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4a0865-5f57-5aac-9e60-dbe69aaa7c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333415} {:answer/id #uuid "db70806a-1a03-5f87-9afd-163b0c228e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334908} {:answer/id #uuid "fce2b562-b937-5bdf-944f-df98ec166dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09071e15-b470-5b7e-9b11-54f49cdedf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333072} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f3f896-e079-54b5-988f-9af076e4c242" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ee60b09-63e9-5076-abae-7337fc233de5" :question/math-academy-id "q-66729" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x-1|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n\nII. $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-1|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-1|.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y=|x-1|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-1|-2.$\n\n![](images/75/753840da17642582f9dbc0debc18f1fa3efcbbb11142894c1dd823ad6c0c630a.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/15/15f1c797cbddd2ac8e2e9c5be72f842e7d1accfc5f619af57b1f35a27e1015c0.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/a1/a100489b49da7b06c98783b1b1c4ec0de9d891fc2b22f4b36b1a0ac2d54fc4f3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124474 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7a8b58-513e-5c23-befb-bf2a0c2d6677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333084} {:answer/id #uuid "40353dfd-44f9-5911-a718-77d6c856581c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333596} {:answer/id #uuid "680fefff-b2a2-53dd-9095-2495312a2ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333944} {:answer/id #uuid "c5946377-6c26-50bf-b6cd-617222e6c9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334705} {:answer/id #uuid "d517b26b-3f62-59f6-936d-776240726217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680fefff-b2a2-53dd-9095-2495312a2ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333944} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b0a3ce3-3f93-5d1d-a683-088b44a950bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa0547e-3ef1-575a-ba36-cf93ffa1c8dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-66778" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=|x+1|-3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in [1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x+1|-3$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the left,  to get $y=|x+1|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x+1|$ by $3$ units down to get $f(x)=|x+1|-3.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b9f56280089991c7db7652d84a777edd5c5e012921f0d48362f4fc3e4fd2a6e.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]$ and $x\\in [1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/fd/fd0b0fe70e009f43e80b0f712b6cfd576deb4c4c0457f61adae04cf7e44f140c.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/58/582ec1960b053731bf201c84d8c58fd4e1734a2b085cd5549a01aace4b600b4a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e69a4aee-6e92-598b-99b5-1d830b6dea54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Correct Statements About a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b04cce7-f7e1-514a-bf46-6bdc2f41e305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333397} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4005dd-77d3-5d4b-a72c-2cf5db39a286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333531} {:answer/id #uuid "52569706-e3d9-5a3d-bc15-02a4fd34fdec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333748} {:answer/id #uuid "60d691ce-29b6-5220-898b-1b6a3afe6708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333869} {:answer/id #uuid "613a72a0-7650-5c25-bf03-340b66e2443b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "613a72a0-7650-5c25-bf03-340b66e2443b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333875} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3deb942-3ce8-54f2-a03d-3da4ced6794e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "deb97145-eaf3-5dcc-a36b-24fae2c52b5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-68754" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is upper triangular\n- $A$ is lower triangular\n- $A+{A}^{T}$ is upper triangular" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries below the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, since $A$ has non-zero entries above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -8 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true, since the result of $A+{A}^{T}$ has all the entries below the main diagonal equal to zero:\n\n$$\nA+{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07749968-3e6b-5e0c-9918-52a6aa1c9845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333062} {:answer/id #uuid "0e392f1c-28e0-5cfd-bcfd-982879cdb5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333114} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd7e2d3-6fbb-5e30-a2a5-7a607ca160e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$M$ and $N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334800} {:answer/id #uuid "e793b20d-ed00-51ff-a572-f50492352b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335010} {:answer/id #uuid "f000cda8-573a-57b5-ad82-601e93e701c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e392f1c-28e0-5cfd-bcfd-982879cdb5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333114} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31efda6-1343-5206-8ccb-2ba28c80e3e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "166c18b6-0458-59df-a57a-4df3b7281df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-69518" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $L$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $M$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "178b7b78-ce61-5ace-b816-0da653a18a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333199} {:answer/id #uuid "47cd1dbc-be71-51be-b2cf-479935b68123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333655} {:answer/id #uuid "5203f619-afc8-5b31-ab01-cfaff6bf135b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333742} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa6b0fe-782f-576d-8e14-2f5d5a7049b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334090} {:answer/id #uuid "82030a72-942d-5492-ba93-cfc08123470f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ and $N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5203f619-afc8-5b31-ab01-cfaff6bf135b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$L$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333742} :answer-field/id #uuid "d528337c-faad-558d-b936-6a50c8cc032c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c563e57f-2913-5048-b8da-281d6ade0aee" :question/math-academy-id "q-69523" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,M=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $L$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $M$ has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 4.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $L$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f092e08-35ca-5f13-9f8c-98d27b461bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333264} {:answer/id #uuid "a2717480-6f9d-59bd-9d7a-82a7fab69cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334415} {:answer/id #uuid "a4df4754-34f0-59b0-81e5-7000872375c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334435} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bfc4cc-9695-569c-bbaf-26b6629dbf66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334727} {:answer/id #uuid "f69e4344-8717-50d5-86e6-98cd74a96746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f092e08-35ca-5f13-9f8c-98d27b461bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333264} :answer-field/id #uuid "b839fdaa-9e23-5da6-892f-6f45111e2102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "924829bc-4eac-54c3-82c8-f773955ca97e" :question/math-academy-id "q-69834" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\n- $M$ is a singular matrix\n- $det(M)=1$\n- $det(M)=0$\n\n \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-4-12)+2(-2+6)+1(4+4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-16)+2\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-16+8+8 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}]}
